---
// ── FINDING A RESOLVED (Leg D4, 2026-10-05): one archive, one door ────────
// The architecture audit parked this as "fixed-income lives at two URLs".
// CANONICAL = /category/fixed-income/ (the FLAT path). This file is no longer
// a second copy of the archive — it is a pointer to the canonical URL, so the
// archive is rendered at exactly ONE address and the nested path carries no
// duplicate content for a crawler to weigh.
//
// Evidence that chose FLAT as canonical (measured, not preference):
//   flat  /category/fixed-income/               26 hrefs / 26 dist pages
//   nested /category/investing/fixed-income/   159 hrefs / 79 dist pages
// The 159 are not 159 independent votes: src/data/site.ts NAV renders TWICE
// per page (desktop .snav-list + mobile .mno-list in layouts/Layout.astro),
// so every page carries the same two nav links. The 26 flat hrefs are the 26
// genuinely distinct entry points that already point at the flat shape:
//   home category chips (index.astro:51), All Articles strip + its paged
//   twin, every sibling category archive's pill-strip, 404, search, the
//   [year] archive tree, and ArticleCard's category badge.
// Three hard constraints closed the question:
//   1. Every one of those 26 flat emitters builds its href from the shared
//      template `category/${c.slug}/` in src/data/site.ts CATEGORIES. Eight
//      of them live outside this leg's lane (components/ArticleCard.astro,
//      pages/index.astro, pages/articles/*, pages/404.astro, pages/search.
//      astro, pages/sitemap.xml.ts). Deleting the flat route would have left
//      8 emitters pointing at a dead address — a 26-link dangling fan-out
//      that cannot be repaired from src/pages/category/**.
//   2. Conversely the nested href is emitted from ONE place, site.ts NAV,
//      and scripts/check-header-fidelity.mjs:78 hard-asserts the literal
//      string "category/investing/fixed-income/" inside site.ts
//      ("nested nav: All Articles > Investing > Fixed Income"). Deleting the
//      nested route means editing that string, and gates are not this leg's
//      to touch. Keeping the route alive is what keeps the gate honest.
//   3. Flat is WP's real category base — the shape the theme already emits
//      everywhere a category is linked — so it is the stable address if the
//      category is ever re-parented. The nested shape bakes the parent
//      /category/investing/ prefix into the URL and breaks on re-parent.
//
// So: the archive stays at the flat URL (catch-all pages/category/[slug].
// astro, unchanged, still 200, same h1 "Fixed Income in Nigeria"), and this
// nested URL degrades to a redirect. The nav's 2 links per page keep working
// — they cost one hop instead of 404ing — and the sitemap still lists both
// (sitemap.xml.ts is not this leg's file; its nested <loc> now resolves to
// the canonical target rather than to duplicate archive HTML).
const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "")}/`;
const origin = (Astro.site?.toString() ?? "https://godschi10.github.io").replace(/\/$/, "");
const target = `${base}category/fixed-income/`;
const targetAbs = `${origin}${target}`;
const label = "Fixed Income";
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-NG">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex,follow" />
    <title>{label} — GWill Finance</title>
    <link rel="canonical" href={targetAbs} />
    <meta http-equiv="refresh" content={`0; url=${target}`} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <h1>{label} has moved</h1>
      <p>This archive now lives at one address only. Taking you there now — if nothing happens, <a href={target}>open the {label} archive</a>.</p>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>